anya: Quick check — hitting "require is not defined" during Vercel Edge build when importing our Node TS SDK. Next 13 edge runtime, SWC/Vite. Any quick pointers?

samir: Edge runtimes are ESM-only, so dynamic require or CJS entrypoints blow up. Make sure package.json has ESM exports and avoid conditional require.

anya: Our package.json currently has "type": "module" but consumers on Node serverless still expect a CJS fallback. I get failures in preview edge funcs.

zara: We solved similar by adding explicit exports mapping (import vs require). E.g.:
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    },
    "./package.json": "./package.json"
  }
}
```
This lets Next/Vercel pick the import (ESM) entry for Edge.

luca: For Vite specifically, also watch out for dependency pre-bundling. If some dep is CJS-only, Vite might SSR-prebundle it and cause a runtime shim. Try adding to vite.config:
```js
optimizeDeps: { exclude: ['our-sdk-cjs-only-dep'] }
ssr: { noExternal: ['our-sdk'] }
```
That helped with weird "require is not defined" errors in dev server.

anya: We also saw streaming responses break: on Edge we tried to return a Node Readable stream and got "pipe is not a function". Any edge-friendly pattern?

samir: Edge uses web streams (ReadableStream/TransformStream). Don't return Node streams directly. Use the Web API: `new Response(new ReadableStream({ start(controller){ ... } }))`. If you need to convert Node stream -> web stream, use `stream.Readable.toWeb(nodeStream)` (node 17+), but avoid polyfills in Edge.

zara: Example we use in SDK for edge-friendly streaming:
```js
export function streamAsResponse(generator) {
  return new Response(new ReadableStream({
    async pull(controller) {
      for await (const chunk of generator()) {
        controller.enqueue(chunk)
      }
      controller.close()
    }
  }))
}
```
No Node-specific APIs, so Vercel Edge/Cloudflare work fine.

luca: Also callout: avoid referencing `process`, `fs`, or `buffer` in top-level module code — those trigger bundlers to include Node shims which break on edge. Use dynamic import inside server-only branches if you must.

anya: What about providing one package for both edge and node? Should we publish separate bundles?

samir: We do dual bundles. Publish a small ESM-only `index.mjs` that is tree-shakeable for Edge (no Node deps), and a CJS/UMD `index.cjs` for Node server/runtime. Use the exports map so bundlers pick correct one. Also add an `edge` field if you want explicit hint, but `exports.import` is usually enough.

zara: For Next specifically, set `edge: true` on the route and ensure the SDK's ESM build doesn't use `globalThis.process.env` at module scope. That caused Next to inject polyfills during compilation.

anya: We also saw Vite dev HMR break when importing `crypto` from Node. Any plugin recs?

luca: Use `vite-plugin-node-stdlib-browser` or alias `crypto` -> `crypto-browserify` but only for browser targets. For Edge, prefer Web Crypto APIs (`crypto.subtle`) when possible.

